WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.167
Scaled dot-product attention can be understood as five operations.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.112
First, project the input into Q, K, and V.

00:00:07.112 --> 00:00:13.872
Second, compare every Query with the relevant Keys using dot products.

00:00:13.872 --> 00:00:17.472
This produces a score matrix: each row corresponds to one Query position,

00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:22.627
and each column corresponds to a Key position.

00:00:22.627 --> 00:00:27.407
Third, divide the scores by the square root of the key dimension.

00:00:27.407 --> 00:00:32.607
Without this scaling, dot products can become large as the vector dimension grows,

00:00:32.607 --> 00:00:34.907
making softmax overly sharp and harder to train.

00:00:34.907 --> 00:00:41.912
Fourth, apply any masks and then softmax so the allowed scores become normalized attention weights.

00:00:41.912 --> 00:00:45.072
Fifth, multiply those weights by V.

00:00:45.072 --> 00:00:50.062
The result is a weighted combination of Value vectors for each token.

00:00:50.062 --> 00:00:56.062
Multi-head attention performs the same general process several times using different learned projections,

00:00:56.062 --> 00:01:04.560
concatenates the head outputs, and uses one more learned projection to return to the model’s hidden dimension.
